{"name":"Whaling Waters","hex":"#2e7176","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"whaling","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The practice of hunting whales.","The practice of spotting whales."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of whale","physically beating."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/whaling"},{"term":"waters","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of water","Amniotic fluid ."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of water"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/waters"}],"entries":[{"title":"Whaling","blocks":[{"text":"Whaling is the hunting of whales for their products such as meat and blubber, which can be turned into a type of oil that was important in the Industrial Revolution. Although it remains unclear where whaling was practiced as an organized industry for the first time, the earliest documentation demonstrating a well-established industry are from the year 1026 from the Basque coastal regions of Spain and France. From there, the whaling industry spread throughout the North Atlantic and later to the rest of the world and became very profitable in terms of trade and resources. Some regions of the world's oceans, along the animals' migration routes, had a particularly dense whale population and became targets for large concentrations of whaling ships, and the industry continued to grow well into the 20th century. The depletion of some whale species to near extinction led to the banning of whaling in many countries by 1969 and to an international cessation of whaling as an industry in the late 1980s."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/To_the_left%2C_the_black-hulled_whaling_ships._To_the_right%2C_the_red-hulled_whale-watching_ship.jpg/330px-To_the_left%2C_the_black-hulled_whaling_ships._To_the_right%2C_the_red-hulled_whale-watching_ship.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaig